<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.salman.jobsearch.selector</string>

    <!-- The decoupled half of the daily flow. com.salman.jobsearch.daily fetches,
         ranks and gates at 08:00 and exits; this job holds the Telegram selection
         open afterwards so the button press can land hours later.

         Deliberately NOT RunAtLoad and NOT KeepAlive=true: the selector bot's
         token allows exactly one getUpdates consumer, so a permanently polling
         process would 409 any manual scripts/telegram_select.py run. It is
         started on demand by run_daily.sh (launchctl kickstart) and exits by
         itself once generation finishes or the 20h window closes.

         Note that omitting RunAtLoad does NOT stop launchd starting this: the
         KeepAlive dict below launches the job as soon as it is bootstrapped.
         Measured, not assumed — bootstrapping an earlier version of this plist
         started it immediately and it posted a real job list to the chat. What
         makes a bare start harmless is the handoff marker: the listener reads
         /tmp/jobsearch_pending_selection.json for the rankset to offer and exits
         0 without sending anything when it is absent. Do not give this process a
         way to guess what to offer. -->

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/Users/salman/Projects/ai-job-search/.venv/bin/python</string>
        <string>/Users/salman/Projects/ai-job-search/scripts/selector_listener.py</string>
    </array>

    <!-- Restart only on abnormal exit. The listener exits 0 for nearly every
         outcome it decides on purpose — generated, window closed, nothing to
         offer, misconfigured, or a previous run interrupted mid-generation —
         precisely because launchd cannot tell a deliberate nonzero exit from a
         crash and would relaunch straight back into the same decision every 10s.

         One deliberate exception, added 2026-08-23: the listener exits 1 when it
         detects that its own polling task has died. That failure is otherwise
         invisible — start_polling polls in a background task, so when its socket
         died on a network transition the process stayed alive, launchd reported
         `state = running` with `last exit code = 0`, and Salman's button taps
         went nowhere for hours. Nonzero is the only signal KeepAlive acts on, and
         relaunching is the right response rather than a loop, because the restart
         does different work: it resumes.

         So nonzero here means: crash, OOM kill, a network teardown that raised,
         or a dead poller. All are cases where the picks are still on disk and
         resuming is correct. On restart the process reloads the state file and
         reattaches to the messages it already sent, sending only the tail it
         never reached, so a reboot mid-selection never double-posts the list. A
         state file marked completed makes the restarted process exit 0 at once,
         so KeepAlive cannot regenerate documents that already exist. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <dict>
        <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
        <false/>
    </dict>

    <!-- launchd's restart floor is 10s regardless; stating it makes the throttle
         explicit rather than incidental. -->
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>10</integer>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/salman/Projects/ai-job-search/logs/daily/selector-stdout.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/salman/Projects/ai-job-search/logs/daily/selector-stderr.log</string>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/salman/Projects/ai-job-search</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <!-- lualatex lives in /Library/TeX/texbin and the drafters shell out to
             it; claude is under the nvm bin. Both must be listed because launchd
             jobs do not inherit a login shell's PATH. -->
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/Users/salman/.nvm/versions/node/v24.19.0/bin:/Users/salman/.bun/bin:/Library/TeX/texbin:/Library/Frameworks/Python.framework/Versions/3.10/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>/Users/salman</string>
        <key>TZ</key>
        <string>Europe/Budapest</string>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
